<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Gervasio Artigas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1764">1764</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as José Artigas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Gervasio Artigas led the rising of the Banda Oriental against Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in 1811 and became leader of the Eastern Province. Demanding independence and a federal union, he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and in 1814–1815 drew the provinces of the Littoral into the Federal League as its Protector. Portuguese invasion from 1816 and defeat at Tacuarembó in 1820 ended his power, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> honours him as national hero.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas.jpg" alt="José Gervasio Artigas" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Manuel Blanes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Blanes_-_Artigas_en_la_Ciudadela.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Gervasio Artigas was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> on 19 June 1764 to Martín José Artigas and Francisca Antonia Arnal, who came from a wealthy family. His parents enrolled him in the Colegio de San Bernardino for religious studies, but he refused its strict discipline. At 12 he moved to the countryside to work on the family's farms, where contact with gauchos and Indians made a great impression on him.</p><p>He later took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> smuggling and was wanted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">hacienda</a> owners and the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-spanish-war-1796-1808">Anglo-Spanish War</a> raised the threat of British invasion, the viceroy negotiated a pardon on condition that he join the Corps of Blandengues, and his military career began in 1797. He was promoted to captain in 1809.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In February 1811 the viceroy Francisco Javier de Elío declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, and the independence movement of the Banda Oriental began. Artigas's forces defeated a Montevidean army at Las Piedras and surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>. When Elío called in Portuguese forces and Buenos Aires signed a truce recognising him, Artigas felt it to be treason, broke with the city and led his followers to Entre Ríos in the Oriental exodus. He insisted on a national organisation on federalist principles.</p><p>In 1814 he organised the League of the Free Peoples and was declared its Protector, and in 1815 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> from the centralist faction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-oriente">Congress of Oriente</a> in 1815 the Oriental Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a>, Entre Ríos, Misiones and Santa Fe declared themselves independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and formed the Federal League. In August 1816 Portugal invaded the Eastern Province, with tacit complicity from Buenos Aires, because the League alarmed Buenos Aires by its federalism and Portugal by its republicanism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-frederico-lecor">Carlos Frederico Lecor</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> on 20 January 1817, and the struggle continued in the countryside for three years. His subordinates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-ramirez">Francisco Ramírez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estanislao-lopez">Estanislao López</a> made agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> against his principles and turned on him. Without men or resources he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> in September 1820, where the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> banished him to Candelaria. He died in Paraguay on 23 September 1850, aged 86. His remains were taken to the Artigas Mausoleum in Montevideo in 1977.</p><p>Artigas was a staunch democrat and federalist, opposed to monarchism and centralism, and drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-paine">Thomas Paine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> he is a national hero, and his birthday on 19 June is a national holiday.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Gervasio Artigas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1811 <b>José Gervasio Artigas</b> rose against Spain and won the Battle of Las Piedras on 18 May; championing federalism against the centralism of Buenos Aires, he took Montevideo in 1815 and organised the Federal League of six provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Eastern Bank the Oriental Revolution of <b>José Gervasio Artigas</b> besieged Montevideo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese forces from Brazil invaded the Banda Oriental in 1816 to crush <b>José Artigas</b>, and General Carlos Frederico Lecor entered Montevideo in January 1817, ending the Eastern Province.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisplatina">Cisplatina</a></li><li><blockquote>The Banda Oriental, east of the Uruguay river, rose against Spanish Montevideo in 1811 under <b>José Gervasio Artigas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-province">Eastern Province</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1814 <b>José Gervasio Artigas</b>, leader of the Eastern Province, drew the provinces of Entre Ríos, Corrientes, Santa Fe and Misiones, and briefly Córdoba, into an alliance against the centralism of Buenos Aires.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-league">Federal League</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating Buenos Aires at Cepeda in 1820 and signing the Treaty of Pilar, Francisco Ramírez broke with his former leader <b>José Artigas</b> of the Federal League and defeated him.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-entre-rios">Republic of Entre Ríos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-34.91,-56.19&amp;year=1819" aria-label="Montevideo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3125 3426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José Gervasio Artigas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3575 3696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-34.91,-56.19&amp;year=1819">What was here in 1819? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
